<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Lit-Hunter: Device Challenge</title>
    
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bungee&family=Roboto+Mono:wght@400;700&family=Share+Tech+Mono&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    
    <script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com"></script>
    
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/tone/14.7.77/Tone.js"></script>

    <style>
        :root {
            --bg-color-dark: #1a123a;
            --bg-color-light: #2c1a4d;
            --primary-glow: #00ffff;
            --secondary-glow: #ff00ff;
            --text-color: #f0f0f0;
            --text-highlight: #ffff00;
        }

        body {
            background-color: #000;
            font-family: 'Roboto Mono', monospace;
            color: var(--text-color);
        }

        #game-container {
            max-width: 900px;
            margin: 2rem auto;
            background-image: linear-gradient(160deg, var(--bg-color-light), var(--bg-color-dark));
            border: 2px solid var(--primary-glow);
            border-radius: 1rem;
            box-shadow: 0 0 25px var(--primary-glow);
            aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            flex-direction: column;
            padding: 2rem;
            position: relative;
            overflow: hidden;
        }

        #game-container::before {
            content: ''; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%;
            background-image: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(0, 255, 255, 0.15) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0, 255, 255, 0.15) 1px, transparent 1px);
            background-size: 40px 40px; opacity: 0.5; z-index: 0;
        }

        h1, h2, .screen { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
        h1 { font-family: 'Bungee', cursive; color: #fff; text-shadow: 0 0 8px var(--primary-glow), 0 0 12px var(--secondary-glow); }
        h2 { font-family: 'Bungee', cursive; color: #fff; }

        .screen { width: 100%; height: 100%; display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; align-items: center; text-align: center; transition: opacity 0.4s ease-in-out; }
        .hidden { display: none !important; }

        .btn {
            font-family: 'Roboto Mono', monospace; font-weight: 700; background-color: transparent; border: 2px solid var(--primary-glow); border-radius: 8px;
            color: var(--primary-glow); padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem; text-shadow: 0 0 5px var(--primary-glow); transition: all 0.2s ease-in-out; cursor: pointer;
            box-shadow: inset 0 0 8px rgba(0, 255, 255, 0.3);
        }

        .btn:hover:not(:disabled) { background-color: var(--primary-glow); color: var(--bg-color-dark); box-shadow: 0 0 15px var(--primary-glow); transform: translateY(-2px) scale(1.03); }
        .btn:disabled { cursor: not-allowed; opacity: 0.4; }

        input[type="text"] {
            font-family: 'Roboto Mono', monospace; background-color: rgba(0,0,0,0.5); border: 2px solid var(--secondary-glow); border-radius: 8px;
            color: var(--text-highlight); text-align: center; padding: 0.5rem; text-transform: uppercase; transition: box-shadow 0.2s ease;
        }
        
        input[type="text"]:focus { outline: none; box-shadow: 0 0 15px var(--secondary-glow); }
        #answers-container button { font-size: 0.8rem; }
        #answers-container button.correct { background-color: #0f0; border-color: #0f0; color: #000; }
        #answers-container button.incorrect { background-color: #f00; border-color: #f00; color: #000; }
        
        #end-game-btn { border-color: var(--secondary-glow); color: var(--secondary-glow); box-shadow: inset 0 0 8px rgba(255, 0, 255, 0.3); text-shadow: 0 0 5px var(--secondary-glow); }
        #end-game-btn:hover:not(:disabled) { background-color: var(--secondary-glow); color: var(--bg-color-dark); box-shadow: 0 0 15px var(--secondary-glow); }

        #fanfare-container { position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; pointer-events: none; z-index: 150; overflow: hidden; }
        .sparkle { position: absolute; width: 10px; height: 10px; background-color: yellow; border-radius: 50%; animation: sparkle-anim 1s ease-out forwards; }
        @keyframes sparkle-anim { 0% { transform: scale(0); opacity: 1; } 50% { transform: scale(1.5); opacity: 0.7; } 100% { transform: scale(0.5) translate(var(--tx), var(--ty)); opacity: 0; } }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div id="game-container">
        <div id="start-screen" class="screen space-y-8">
            <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl">Lit-Hunter: Device Challenge</h1>
            <div class="space-y-4">
                <label for="player-initials" class="text-lg">Enter Your Initials</label>
                <input type="text" id="player-initials" maxlength="3" class="block mx-auto w-24 text-2xl">
            </div>
            <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row space-y-4 md:space-y-0 md:space-x-4">
                <button id="start-game-btn" class="btn text-xl">New Game</button>
                <button id="continue-game-btn" class="btn text-xl hidden">Continue</button>
                <button id="view-scores-btn" class="btn text-xl">High Scores</button>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div id="game-screen" class="screen hidden flex flex-col justify-between w-full h-full">
            <div id="top-bar" class="flex justify-between w-full text-lg font-bold">
                <div id="score">Score: 0</div>
                <div id="streak">Streak: 0</div>
            </div>
            <div id="question-area" class="flex-grow flex flex-col justify-center items-center space-y-4 w-full">
                <p id="question-text" class="text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed"></p>
                <div id="answers-container" class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 w-full max-w-4xl"></div>
            </div>
            <div id="bottom-bar" class="flex justify-between items-center w-full text-sm">
                <div id="question-counter" class="font-bold">Q: 1/117</div>
                <div class="flex space-x-2">
                    <button id="skip-btn" class="btn">Skip (-10)</button>
                    <button id="pause-game-btn" class="btn">Pause</button>
                    <button id="end-game-btn" class="btn">End Game</button>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        
        <div id="highscore-screen" class="screen hidden space-y-6">
            <h1 class="text-6xl">High Scores</h1>
            <ul id="highscore-list" class="text-2xl space-y-2 text-left w-full max-w-md"></ul>
            <button id="main-menu-btn-scores" class="btn text-xl">Main Menu</button>
        </div>

        <div id="game-over-screen" class="screen hidden space-y-6 text-center">
            <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl text-yellow-300">Congratulations!</h1>
            <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl">You have triumphed over LITERATURE!</p>
            <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl text-secondary-glow font-bold" style="text-shadow: 0 0 8px var(--secondary-glow);">YOU ARE THE LORD OF LIT!</p>
            <button id="view-final-scores-btn" class="btn text-xl mt-4">View High Scores</button>
        </div>

        <div id="fanfare-container"></div>
    </div>

    <script>
        const allQuestions = [
            { question: "In Shirley Jackson's short story \"The Lottery,\" how does the following quote display and use external conflict?", quote: "\"'It isn't fair, it isn't right,' Mrs. Hutchinson screamed, and then they were upon her.\"", answers: ["The quote showcases the final, desperate cry of an individual against the brutal, collective tradition of her society.", "The quote shows Mrs. Hutchinson in a conflict with her husband over who should have drawn the slip of paper.", "The quote demonstrates an internal conflict, where Mrs. Hutchinson is fighting against her own fear.", "The quote highlights a conflict between the village and the forces of nature."], correctAnswer: "The quote showcases the final, desperate cry of an individual against the brutal, collective tradition of her society." },
            { question: "In \"The Lottery\" by Shirley Jackson, how does the following quote use indirect characterization?", quote: "\"Although the villagers had forgotten the ritual and lost the original black box, they still remembered to use stones.\"", answers: ["The quote shows through the villagers' actions that they are blindly adherent to violence, valuing the brutal core of the tradition over its meaning.", "The quote shows that the villagers are peaceful and would rather forget the violent parts of the lottery.", "The quote characterizes the villagers as resourceful and innovative, finding new ways to carry out old traditions.", "The quote shows that the villagers are sentimental and care deeply about preserving every aspect of their history."], correctAnswer: "The quote shows through the villagers' actions that they are blindly adherent to violence, valuing the brutal core of the tradition over its meaning." },
            { question: "In \"The Monkey's Paw\" by W.W. Jacobs, what does the following quote indirectly reveal about Mrs. White's character?", quote: "\"'Go and get it and wish,' she cried, and her voice was sharp with pain. 'Oh, my boy, my boy!'\"", answers: ["Her desperate speech shows she is a mother overwhelmed by grief, driven by emotion rather than caution.", "Her command to \"go and get it\" reveals that she is a greedy and materialistic person.", "Her sharp tone shows that she is angry with her husband for making the first wish.", "Her crying reveals that she is too weak to handle the situation and wants others to solve it for her."], correctAnswer: "Her desperate speech shows she is a mother overwhelmed by grief, driven by emotion rather than caution." },
            { question: "In W.W. Jacobs's \"The Monkey's Paw,\" how does the imagery in the final line contribute to the story's mood?", quote: "\"The street lamp flickering opposite shone on a quiet and deserted road.\"", answers: ["It creates a stark visual of emptiness and silence, resulting in a haunting and desolate final impression.", "It creates a peaceful and calm mood, suggesting that the family has found closure.", "It creates a romantic mood, suggesting the return of a loved one.", "It creates a suspenseful mood, hinting that the danger has not yet passed."], correctAnswer: "It creates a stark visual of emptiness and silence, resulting in a haunting and desolate final impression." },
            { question: "In Kate Chopin's \"Story of an Hour,\" what does the \"new spring life\" outside the window symbolize for Mrs. Mallard?", quote: "\"She could see in the open square before her house the tops of trees that were all aquiver with the new spring life.\"", answers: ["It is a symbol of Mrs. Mallard's own awakening and burgeoning sense of freedom and rebirth.", "It symbolizes the life she will miss now that her husband is gone.", "It symbolizes the natural world, which is indifferent to her personal tragedy.", "It is a symbol of the memories of her husband and the life they shared."], correctAnswer: "It is a symbol of Mrs. Mallard's own awakening and burgeoning sense of freedom and rebirth." },
            { question: "In \"Story of an Hour\" by Kate Chopin, how does the phrase \"the joy that kills\" function as a metaphor from the reader's perspective?", quote: "\"When the doctors came they said she had died of heart disease-of the joy that kills.\"", answers: ["It is a tragic metaphor for the death of the newfound joy and freedom she had just discovered.", "It is a metaphor for the literal shock of seeing her husband alive, which was too much for her weak heart.", "It is a metaphor for a peaceful and happy death, which the doctors believed she had.", "It is a metaphor for the oppressive nature of her marriage, which had been slowly killing her."], correctAnswer: "It is a tragic metaphor for the death of the newfound joy and freedom she had just discovered." },
            { question: "In Nathaniel Hawthorne's story \"Dr. Heidegger's Experiment,\" what does the revived rose primarily act as a motif for?", quote: "\"The crushed and dried rose symbolized his past life... When the water revives it, it symbolizes the possibility of reviving youth itself.\"", answers: ["It represents the central theme of the fleeting and delicate nature of youth and beauty.", "It represents Dr. Heidegger's scientific genius and success.", "It represents the wealth and vanity of the four guests.", "It represents the eternal nature of true love."], correctAnswer: "It represents the central theme of the fleeting and delicate nature of youth and beauty." },
            { question: "In \"Dr. Heidegger's Experiment\" by Nathaniel Hawthorne, how does the guests' final decision function as a form of indirect characterization?", quote: "\"They resolved forthwith to make a pilgrimage to Florida, and quaff at morning, noon, and night, from the Fountain of Youth.\"", answers: ["Their resolution to find the Fountain shows they are foolish and incapable of learning from experience.", "Their decision characterizes them as adventurous and brave.", "Their desire for more water shows they are grateful to Dr. Heidegger for his help.", "Their pilgrimage plan characterizes them as deeply religious and spiritual."], correctAnswer: "Their resolution to find the Fountain shows they are foolish and incapable of learning from experience." },
            { question: "In Ernest Hemingway's \"Old Man at the Bridge,\" how does the narrator's assessment of the old man's luck serve as direct characterization?", quote: "\"That and the fact that cats know how to look after themselves was all the good luck that old man would ever have.\"", answers: ["It is a blunt assessment that directly defines the old man by his utter helplessness and seals his fate.", "It directly characterizes the narrator as a cynical and pessimistic person.", "It directly characterizes the old man as a lucky person who has survived many hardships.", "It is a direct statement about the intelligence and resilience of cats."], correctAnswer: "It is a blunt assessment that directly defines the old man by his utter helplessness and seals his fate." },
            { question: "In \"Old Man at the Bridge\" by Ernest Hemingway, how does the old man's simple statement reveal his internal conflict?", quote: "\"I was only taking care of animals.\"", answers: ["It shows his conflict comes from his sense of duty to the animals he abandoned, which causes him more pain than the war itself.", "It reveals his internal struggle with feeling useless during the war.", "It shows he is conflicted about whether he should have fought in the war or stayed with his animals.", "It reveals his internal conflict over his love for cats versus his love for pigeons and goats."], correctAnswer: "It shows his conflict comes from his sense of duty to the animals he abandoned, which causes him more pain than the war itself." },
            { question: "In Ray Bradbury's \"The Dragon,\" how does the knights' dialogue in this quote work as indirect characterization?", quote: "\"A beast of the soul, then, and we will kill it!\"", answers: ["Their words characterize them as brave and noble, yet tragically out of touch with the new reality they face.", "Their speech characterizes them as fearful and hesitant to face the dragon.", "Their dialogue shows they are greedy and only interested in the treasure the dragon guards.", "Their statement reveals they are scientists trying to understand a new phenomenon."], correctAnswer: "Their words characterize them as brave and noble, yet tragically out of touch with the new reality they face." },
            { question: "In \"The Dragon\" by Ray Bradbury, how is the train whistle an example of personification?", quote: "\"The train whistle screamed... It was gone. The land was empty. The dragon was gone.\"", answers: ["The whistle is given the human action of screaming, which gives the machine a monstrous, predatory quality.", "The whistle is described as being empty, which is a human emotion.", "The whistle is compared to a dragon, which is a mythical creature, not a person.", "The land is described as being empty, personifying it with a sense of loss."], correctAnswer: "The whistle is given the human action of screaming, which gives the machine a monstrous, predatory quality." },
            { question: "In William Golding's Lord of the Flies, what does the destruction of the conch symbolize in this moment?", quote: "\"...the conch exploded into a thousand white fragments and ceased to exist.\"", answers: ["It symbolizes the complete triumph of savagery over civilization and order on the island.", "It symbolizes the boys' anger at Piggy for being annoying.", "It symbolizes the fragility of nature and the boys' carelessness.", "It symbolizes the end of the boys' hope of ever being rescued."], correctAnswer: "It symbolizes the complete triumph of savagery over civilization and order on the island." },
            { question: "In Lord of the Flies by William Golding, how does the mask function as a symbol in this quote?", quote: "\"...the mask was a thing on its own, behind which Jack hid, liberated from shame and self-consciousness.\"", answers: ["The mask is a symbol of savagery and the loss of individual identity, freeing Jack from the rules of society.", "The mask symbolizes Jack's creativity and artistic skill.", "The mask is a symbol of unity, allowing the boys to feel like part of a cohesive tribe.", "The mask symbolizes Jack's desire to be a leader and his insecurity about his appearance."], correctAnswer: "The mask is a symbol of savagery and the loss of individual identity, freeing Jack from the rules of society." },
            { question: "In William Golding's Lord of the Flies, how is \"the darkness of man's heart\" used as a metaphor?", quote: "\"Ralph wept for the end of innocence, the darkness of man's heart, and the fall through the air of the true, wise friend called Piggy.\"", answers: ["It is a metaphor for the inherent evil and savagery that is a part of human nature.", "It is a metaphor for the physical darkness of the jungle at night.", "It is a metaphor for the boys' ignorance and lack of education.", "It is a metaphor for the sadness and grief Ralph feels after Piggy's death."], correctAnswer: "It is a metaphor for the inherent evil and savagery that is a part of human nature." },
            { question: "In the plot of George Orwell's Animal Farm, what does the selling of Boxer represent?", quote: "\"'They are taking Boxer to the knacker's!' cried Benjamin.\"", answers: ["It is a critical turning point that represents the ultimate betrayal of the farm's ideals and the corruption of the pigs.", "It represents a necessary sacrifice for the good of the farm's economy.", "It represents the end of the external conflict with the humans.", "It is the resolution of the story, where all the animals are finally sold."], correctAnswer: "It is a critical turning point that represents the ultimate betrayal of the farm's ideals and the corruption of the pigs." },
            { question: "In Animal Farm by George Orwell, what does the act of a pig walking on two legs symbolize?", quote: "\"It was a pig walking on his hind legs.\"", answers: ["It symbolizes the pigs' ultimate transformation into their former human oppressors, breaking the most fundamental rule of Animalism.", "It symbolizes the intelligence and evolutionary progress of the pigs.", "It symbolizes a moment of celebration and triumph for all the animals on the farm.", "It symbolizes the pigs' desire to be more efficient workers."], correctAnswer: "It symbolizes the pigs' ultimate transformation into their former human oppressors, breaking the most fundamental rule of Animalism." },
            { question: "In George Orwell's Animal Farm, how does the final scene conclude a recurring motif?", quote: "\"The creatures outside looked from pig to man... but already it was impossible to say which was which.\"", answers: ["It concludes the motif of pigs gradually adopting human behaviors, solidifying their total corruption.", "It concludes the motif of the animals' unity and friendship.", "It resolves the motif of the animals' struggle against the hardships of farming.", "It resolves the motif of Benjamin's cynicism by showing he was wrong about the pigs."], correctAnswer: "It concludes the motif of pigs gradually adopting human behaviors, solidifying their total corruption." },
            { question: "In William Shakespeare's Macbeth, how does the hallucinatory dagger function as a symbol in Macbeth's soliloquy?", quote: "\"Is this a dagger which I see before me, / The handle toward my hand?\"", answers: ["It symbolizes both Macbeth's bloody ambition and his guilty conscience regarding his impending crime.", "It is a symbol of the witches' curse and their direct control over his actions.", "It symbolizes a real weapon that appears through magic to help him.", "It is a symbol of King Duncan's royal power and authority."], correctAnswer: "It symbolizes both Macbeth's bloody ambition and his guilty conscience regarding his impending crime." },
            { question: "In Macbeth by William Shakespeare, how does Lady Macbeth's line about the \"perfumes of Arabia\" demonstrate hyperbole?", quote: "\"All the perfumes of Arabia will not sweeten this little hand.\"", answers: ["It is a deliberate exaggeration used to express the immeasurable and eternal nature of her guilt.", "It is a literal statement about the weakness of Arabian perfumes.", "It is a sarcastic comment meant to mock Macbeth's earlier guilt over the blood on his hands.", "It is an exaggeration used to show her frustration with the servants for not cleaning properly."], correctAnswer: "It is a deliberate exaggeration used to express the immeasurable and eternal nature of her guilt." },
            { question: "In William Shakespeare's Macbeth, what is the primary function of the metaphors in Macbeth's \"walking shadow\" speech?", quote: "\"Life's but a walking shadow, a poor player / That struts and frets his hour upon the stage...\"", answers: ["He compares life to an insubstantial shadow and a brief stage performance to express his despair and feeling that life is meaningless.", "He uses the metaphors to devise a plan to trick his enemies.", "He compares life to a shadow and an actor to express his desire to be a performer.", "He uses the metaphors to criticize the actors performing at his castle."], correctAnswer: "He compares life to an insubstantial shadow and a brief stage performance to express his despair and feeling that life is meaningless." },
            { question: "In Mary Shelley's Frankenstein, how does the Creature's quote demonstrate his internal conflict?", quote: "\"Did I request thee, Maker, from my clay / To mould Me man, did I solicit thee / From darkness to promote me?\"", answers: ["He is tormented by the injustice of being created only to be abandoned and left to suffer.", "He is conflicted about whether he should seek revenge on Victor or try to find a new life.", "He is struggling to understand the scientific principles that led to his creation.", "He is in conflict with society's rules and expectations."], correctAnswer: "He is tormented by the injustice of being created only to be abandoned and left to suffer." },
            { question: "In Frankenstein by Mary Shelley, how does the Creature use the figures of Adam and \"the fallen angel\" as metaphors for his own identity?", quote: "\"I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel...\"", answers: ["He uses them to describe his fall from a state of potential innocence to one of vengeful misery.", "He uses them to suggest that he has supernatural powers.", "He uses them to compare his physical strength to that of biblical figures.", "He uses them to mock Victor's belief in religion."], correctAnswer: "He uses them to describe his fall from a state of potential innocence to one of vengeful misery." },
            { question: "In Mary Shelley's Frankenstein, how does the Creature's final speech serve as the novel's plot resolution?", quote: "\"Soon these burning miseries will be extinct. I shall ascend my funeral pile triumphantly...\"", answers: ["His declared intention to die ends the cycle of revenge and resolves the central conflict between him and his creator.", "It resolves the plot by revealing that the Creature has finally found happiness.", "It is the climax of the story, where he finally confronts Victor.", "It introduces a new plot where the Creature seeks to create a companion for himself."], correctAnswer: "His declared intention to die ends the cycle of revenge and resolves the central conflict between him and his creator." },
            { question: "In \"The Cask of Amontillado\" by Edgar Allan Poe, how does Montresor's reply to Fortunato's plea serve as indirect characterization?", quote: "\"'For the love of God, Montresor!' 'Yes,' I said, 'for the love of God!'\"", answers: ["His chilling, ironic repetition of the plea characterizes him as utterly remorseless and sadistic.", "His reply characterizes him as a deeply religious man who believes his actions are divinely justified.", "His answer shows that he is having second thoughts and feels guilty for his actions.", "His speech characterizes him as confused and not fully aware of what he is doing."], correctAnswer: "His chilling, ironic repetition of the plea characterizes him as utterly remorseless and sadistic." },
            { question: "In Saki's The Interlopers, how does the following quote demonstrate a resolution to the characters' internal conflict?", quote: "\"And each prayed a private prayer that his men might be the first to arrive, so that he might be the first to show honorable attention to the enemy that had become a friend.\"", answers: ["It reveals their lifelong hatred has been completely replaced by a newfound desire for reconciliation and honor.", "It shows they are still conflicted, as each man secretly hopes his own men will capture the other.", "It highlights their internal struggle between family duty and personal feelings.", "The prayer is a sign of their internal fear of dying alone in the forest."], correctAnswer: "It reveals their lifelong hatred has been completely replaced by a newfound desire for reconciliation and honor." },
            { question: "In The Interlopers by Saki, how does this single word function as the story's shocking plot resolution?", quote: "\"Wolves.\"", answers: ["The word is an ironic resolution, making the men's reconciliation meaningless as they face a new, insurmountable external conflict.", "It is the story's climax, showing the final battle between the men and the forces of nature.", "It is a cliffhanger, leaving the reader to wonder if the men will be rescued in time.", "It resolves the plot by having the wolves rescue the men and lead them to safety."], correctAnswer: "The word is an ironic resolution, making the men's reconciliation meaningless as they face a new, insurmountable external conflict." },
            { question: "In Guy de Maupassant's The Necklace, how does this passage use indirect characterization to show Mathilde's transformation?", quote: "\"She had become the woman of impoverished households—strong and hard and rough. With frowsy hair, with skirts awry, and with red hands, she talked loud while washing the floor...\"", answers: ["Her changed looks and actions show the brutal, physical toll that a decade of poverty and hard labor has taken on her.", "It shows that she has become a more confident and outspoken person.", "It characterizes her as lazy and careless about her appearance.", "It reveals that she has secretly enjoyed the simple life of a working-class woman."], correctAnswer: "Her changed looks and actions show the brutal, physical toll that a decade of poverty and hard labor has taken on her." },
            { question: "In The Necklace by Guy de Maupassant, what does the fake necklace come to symbolize by the end of the story?", quote: "\"'Oh, my poor Mathilde! Why, my necklace was paste. It was worth at most five hundred francs!'\"", answers: ["It symbolizes the deceptive nature of appearances and the vanity that led to Mathilde's ruin.", "It symbolizes the true friendship between Mathilde and Madame Forestier.", "It symbolizes the wealth and high social status that Mathilde always desired.", "It symbolizes the harsh economic realities of 19th-century France."], correctAnswer: "It symbolizes the deceptive nature of appearances and the vanity that led to Mathilde's ruin." },
            { question: "In O. Henry's The Gift of the Magi, what does Della's action and dialogue indirectly characterize her as?", quote: "\"'You've cut off your hair?' asked Jim... 'Cut it off and sold it,' said Della. 'Don't you like me just as well, anyhow? I'm me without my hair, ain't I?'\"", answers: ["She is characterized as selfless and deeply in love, showing her identity is tied to Jim's love, not her appearance.", "She is characterized as impulsive and careless, not thinking about the consequences of her actions.", "She is characterized as insecure, needing Jim's constant approval.", "She is characterized as resentful that she had to sell her hair for him."], correctAnswer: "She is characterized as selfless and deeply in love, showing her identity is tied to Jim's love, not her appearance." },
            { question: "In The Gift of the Magi by O. Henry, how does the narrator use a metaphor to convey the story's theme in the final line?", quote: "\"But in a last word to the wise of these days let it be said that of all who give gifts these two were the wisest... They are the magi.\"", answers: ["The narrator compares Jim and Della to the biblical Magi to suggest their selfless, sacrificial love makes their gifts the wisest of all.", "The metaphor suggests that Jim and Della are royalty.", "The metaphor is used to imply that the gifts Jim and Della gave were expensive and rare.", "The narrator uses the metaphor to criticize the couple for their foolish and impractical gifts."], correctAnswer: "The narrator compares Jim and Della to the biblical Magi to suggest their selfless, sacrificial love makes their gifts the wisest of all." },
            { question: "In Richard Connell's The Most Dangerous Game, how does this exchange of dialogue establish the story's external conflict?", quote: "\"'Hunting? General Zaroff, what you speak of is murder.'\"", answers: ["It establishes the primary conflict between Rainsford's moral code and Zaroff's depraved \"game\".", "It shows an internal conflict within Rainsford as he debates the ethics of hunting.", "It creates a conflict between General Zaroff and his servant, Ivan.", "It introduces a conflict between the hunters and the island's dangerous animals."], correctAnswer: "It establishes the primary conflict between Rainsford's moral code and Zaroff's depraved \"game\"." },
            { question: "In The Most Dangerous Game by Richard Connell, what does Rainsford's final thought indirectly characterize him as?", quote: "\"He had never slept in a better bed, Rainsford decided.\"", answers: ["It characterizes him as a changed man who has become as ruthless as the man he hunted.", "It shows he is relieved the hunt is over and can finally rest.", "It characterizes him as someone who appreciates luxury and comfort.", "It suggests he feels guilty and is trying to forget what he has done."], correctAnswer: "It characterizes him as a changed man who has become as ruthless as the man he hunted." },
            { question: "In William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet, how does Romeo use a metaphor to describe Juliet?", quote: "\"But, soft! what light through yonder window breaks? / It is the east, and Juliet is the sun.\"", answers: ["He compares her to the sun, meaning she is the center of his world, bringing light that banishes his darkness.", "He compares her to the sun to say she has a fiery and angry personality.", "He uses the metaphor to suggest she is distant and unreachable.", "He compares her to the sun because her bedroom is brightly lit."], correctAnswer: "He compares her to the sun, meaning she is the center of his world, bringing light that banishes his darkness." },
