            { question: "In Of Mice and Men by John Steinbeck, how does George's final action in the novel serve as indirect characterization?", quote: "\"He pulled the trigger... Lennie jarred, and then settled slowly forward to the sand, and he lay without quivering.\"", answers: ["The act, though horrific, characterizes him as merciful and courageous for sparing Lennie a more brutal fate from the lynch mob.", "The action characterizes him as a cold-blooded and selfish killer.", "It shows that he is a weak character who gave in to pressure from the other men.", "It characterizes him as impulsive and prone to fits of anger."], correctAnswer: "The act, though horrific, characterizes him as merciful and courageous for sparing Lennie a more brutal fate from the lynch mob." },
            { question: "In John Steinbeck's Of Mice and Men, what does the dead puppy symbolize in this scene?", quote: "\"'Why do you got to get killed? You ain't so little as mice. I didn't bounce you hard.'\"", answers: ["It symbolizes Lennie's inability to control his own strength and serves as a grim omen for what will happen to Curley's wife.", "The puppy symbolizes the death of the American Dream for Lennie and George.", "It symbolizes the cruelty of the world and the loss of innocence.", "The puppy is a symbol of Lennie's desire for a friend."], correctAnswer: "It symbolizes Lennie's inability to control his own strength and serves as a grim omen for what will happen to Curley's wife." },
